24. Experimental Design (Theoretical Specification)

We specify, but do not execute, a rigorous experimental protocol.

24.1 Baselines

· A: Standard LLM prompt: "Generate five important research questions about domain X."
· B: Literature-conditioned LLM.
· C: AI research agent (e.g., AI Co-Scientist adapted for question generation).
· D: Ablation: No Gap Memory (gaps detected on-the-fly, not stored).
· E: Ablation: No adversarial evaluation.
· F: Ablation: Flat gap representation (untyped, no falsification conditions).

24.2 Metrics

· GapPrecision: expert-annotated fraction of questions traceable to genuine gaps.
· RediscoveryRate: fraction corresponding to already-known questions.
· StructuralDiversity: embedding-space dispersion plus coverage of gap types.
· Testability: blind expert judgment.
· Falsifiability: can the system identify observations capable of disproving the hypothesis?

24.3 Protocol Details

· Sample size: N = 50 questions per system.
· Expert pool: 5 domain experts, blind to condition.
· Inter-rater reliability: Fleiss's kappa ≥ 0.6 required.
· Statistical analysis: mixed-effects model with system as fixed effect, expert as random effect.
· Multiple comparisons: apply Bonferroni correction.

Note on statistical power: Before finalizing N=50, a power analysis must be conducted to ensure that the expected effect sizes (e.g., the difference in GapPrecision between the full architecture and Baseline D) are detectable at the Bonferroni-corrected significance level. If not, the sample size must be increased or the comparison set reduced. This will be part of the pre-registration for any future empirical study.

This protocol is pre-registered; no experiments are run in this thesis.

