Appendix A: Formal Foundations for Knowledge Graph and Epistemic Memories

A.1 Knowledge Graph

A Knowledge Graph is a tuple

KG = (V, E, \ell_V, \ell_E, \tau, C)
\]  

where:

· V is a finite set of nodes (entities, concepts, observations).
· E \subseteq V \times V is a set of directed edges representing relations.
· \ell_V : V \to \mathcal{L}_V assigns a label from a finite set \mathcal{L}_V to each node.
· \ell_E : E \to \mathcal{L}_E assigns a relation type from a finite set \mathcal{L}_E (e.g., causes, predicts, contradicts, supports, depends_on, correlates_with, analogous_to, fails_under, unexplained_by).
· \tau : E \to \mathcal{S} assigns an epistemic status from
  \mathcal{S} = \{\text{OBSERVED}, \text{SUPPORTED}, \text{CORRELATED}, \text{CAUSAL}, \text{THEORETICAL}, \text{SPECULATIVE}, \text{CONTRADICTED}, \text{UNKNOWN}\}.
· C is a set of conditions; each edge e \in E may be annotated with a condition c_e \in C under which the relation holds.

The KG evolves over time: KG_t denotes the state at time t.

A.2 Gap Tuple

A gap g is a tuple

g = (T, A, E, F, R, P, t, h)
\]  

as defined in Section 3.1. The predicates F and R are expressed in a formal language \Phi over KG states. We assume \Phi is closed under Boolean connectives and contains atomic predicates of the form “node v has label l”, “edge (u,v) has type t”, “there exists a node m with property …”, etc. The semantics is the standard satisfaction relation \models. We write R(KG) (resp. F(KG)) to mean that predicate R (resp. F) evaluates to true in state KG.

A.3 Gap Memory and Question Memory

· Gap Memory M_G is a finite set of gaps, with the invariant that for every g \in M_G, g is neither resolved nor dissolved in the current KG state (see Appendix B for formal definitions).
· Question Memory M_Q is a set of question records
  q_{\text{rec}} = (q, \text{status}, \text{history}, \text{timestamp})
  \]  
  where q is a question object (Appendix C), status \in \{\text{generated}, \text{refined}, \text{accepted}, \text{rejected}, \text{resolved}, \text{abandoned}\}, and history is a list of modifications.

