Appendix B: Gap Algebra — Formal Semantics and Proofs

B.1 Resolution and Dissolution

For a gap g = (T,A,E,F,R,P,t,h) and a KG state KG:

· g is resolved in KG iff R(KG) = \top.
· g is dissolved in KG iff F(KG) = \top.

We assume the following Axiom of Mutual Exclusivity for every gap g:

\forall KG,\; \neg(F(KG) \land R(KG)).
\]  

This encodes that a gap cannot simultaneously be shown to be non‑genuine and be answered. The axiom is enforced when constructing gap predicates; if a conflict arises, the gap is flagged as malformed and rejected.

B.2 Subsumption

For gaps g_1, g_2, we define

g_1 \preceq g_2 \quad \text{iff} \quad \forall KG,\; R_2(KG) \Rightarrow R_1(KG).
\]  

That is, resolving g_2 necessarily resolves g_1.

Proposition B.1. The relation \preceq is a preorder (reflexive and transitive).

Proof.
Reflexivity: R(KG) \Rightarrow R(KG) trivially.
Transitivity: If g_1 \preceq g_2 and g_2 \preceq g_3, then for any KG,

R_3(KG) \Rightarrow R_2(KG) \Rightarrow R_1(KG),
\]  

hence g_1 \preceq g_3. ∎

Proposition B.2. If g_1 \preceq g_2 and g_2 is resolved in KG, then g_1 is resolved in KG.

Proof. Immediate from the definition of \preceq. ∎

B.3 Composition

Given gaps g_1 = (\ldots, F_1, R_1, \ldots) and g_2 = (\ldots, F_2, R_2, \ldots), their composition g_1 \otimes g_2 is a new gap g_3 whose predicates are defined by:

R_3(KG) \equiv R_1(KG) \land R_2(KG), \qquad
F_3(KG) \equiv F_1(KG) \lor F_2(KG).

The other fields (type, anchors, evidence, provenance, timestamps) are merged according to a deterministic rule (e.g., union of anchors, concatenation of provenance, type = Composite). The composition is well‑defined because Boolean formulas are closed under conjunction and disjunction.

Proposition B.3. Composition is associative up to logical equivalence of predicates: (g_1 \otimes g_2) \otimes g_3 \equiv g_1 \otimes (g_2 \otimes g_3).

Proof. Let g_{12} = g_1 \otimes g_2 and g_{23} = g_2 \otimes g_3. Then

· For resolution:
  R_{(12)3} = (R_1 \land R_2) \land R_3 = R_1 \land (R_2 \land R_3) = R_{1(23)}.
· For falsification:
  F_{(12)3} = (F_1 \lor F_2) \lor F_3 = F_1 \lor (F_2 \lor F_3) = F_{1(23)}.

Hence the two composite gaps have logically equivalent predicates. ∎

Proposition B.4. If g_3 = g_1 \otimes g_2 and g_3 is dissolved in KG, then at least one of g_1 or g_2 is dissolved in KG.

Proof. By definition, F_3(KG) = F_1(KG) \lor F_2(KG). If F_3(KG) = \top, then either F_1(KG) = \top or F_2(KG) = \top (or both). Thus at least one component is dissolved. ∎

Corollary B.5. If g_3 = g_1 \otimes g_2 is dissolved, then it is not resolved (by mutual exclusivity applied to g_3).

B.4 Evolution

Evolution is a mapping

\text{evolve}: \mathcal{G} \times \Delta KG \to \mathcal{G}
\]  

where \Delta KG denotes the changes (additions, deletions, modifications) applied to the KG. The evolution of a gap updates its anchors, evidence, and possibly its predicates F,R to reflect new knowledge, while preserving the gap’s identity (a unique ID). We do not impose further axioms; the operation is deterministic given the KG update.

B.5 Invariant of Gap Memory

The system maintains the following invariant:

Invariant B.6. For every gap g \in M_G and current KG state KG, neither R(g,KG) nor F(g,KG) is true.

Proof (by induction on loop iterations). Initially M_G = \emptyset. At each iteration, new gaps are added only after passing the Gap Critic (Section 8), which rejects candidates that are already resolved (i.e., R true) or dissolved (i.e., F true) relative to the current KG. Existing gaps are re‑checked after each KG update: if R becomes true, the gap is removed and marked resolved; if F becomes true, it is removed and marked dissolved. Thus the invariant holds after every step. ∎

