Appendix C: Question Formation Operators — Formal Semantics

A question object is a tuple

q = (id, text, gap\_ref, operator, type, sig)
\]  

where id is a unique identifier, text is a natural language projection, gap\_ref points to the originating gap g \in M_G, operator \in \mathcal{O} is the operator used, type \in \{\text{abductive}, \text{causal}, \text{boundary}, \text{counterfactual}, \text{inverse}, \text{transfer}, \text{decomposition}\}, and sig is a structural signature (Appendix H).

The operators are functions

\mathcal{O}_i : \mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Q})
\]  

mapping a gap to a finite set of candidate questions. We define each operator precisely:

1. Abduction (i = \text{abd}): Applicable to gaps of type MissingMechanism. If the gap anchors contain an edge A \xrightarrow{\text{empirically-observed}} B with no known mechanism m, then
   \text{Abduction}(g) = \{ q \mid text(q) = \text{“What mechanism explains } A \to B \text{?”}, sig(q) = \{A,B,\text{unexplained}\} \}.
   If multiple such edges exist, one question per edge is generated.
2. Causality (i = \text{caus}): For gaps of type Anomaly or Contradiction, where a predictor X and outcome Y are involved, produce
   \text{Causality}(g) = \{ q \mid text(q) = \text{“Does } X \text{ cause } Y \text{ under condition } C \text{?”}, sig(q) = \{X,Y,C,\text{causal}\} \}.
3. Boundary (i = \text{bnd}): For Boundary gaps with model M valid on domain D but failing on adjacent B, produce
   \text{Boundary}(g) = \{ q \mid text(q) = \text{“At what conditions does model } M \text{ fail?”}, sig(q) = \{M,D,B,\text{boundary}\} \}.
4. Counterfactual (i = \text{cf}): For any gap, if there is an entity X with property P, produce
   \text{Counterfactual}(g) = \{ q \mid text(q) = \text{“What would happen if } X \text{ were changed?”}, sig(q) = \{X,\text{counterfactual}\} \}.
5. Inverse (i = \text{inv}): Given a target outcome Y, produce
   \text{Inverse}(g) = \{ q \mid text(q) = \text{“What conditions would be required to produce } Y \text{?”}, sig(q) = \{Y,\text{inverse}\} \}.
6. Transfer (i = \text{tr}): For CrossDomain gaps, if a mechanism M_A in domain A is structurally similar to a problem P_B in domain B, produce
   \text{Transfer}(g) = \{ q \mid text(q) = \text{“Can mechanism } M_A \text{ explain/solve } P_B \text{?”}, sig(q) = \{M_A, P_B, \text{transfer}\} \}.
7. Decomposition (i = \text{dec}): For any gap, produce sub‑gaps g_1, \ldots, g_k (using the Gap Hunter recursively) and for each sub‑gap produce a question via an appropriate operator. The resulting questions have sig equal to the sub‑gap’s signature.

Proposition C.1. For any gap g, applying any operator yields a finite set of questions, and each question references g.

Proof. The gap tuple is finite (anchors A and evidence E are finite sets). Each operator constructs at most one question per element of a finite collection (e.g., edges in anchors, entities in anchors), hence the output is finite. The gap\_ref field is set to g by construction. ∎

