Appendix D: Novelty and Accessibility — Formal Definitions and Properties

Let \mathcal{Q} be the set of all possible questions. We assume the existence of two functions:

· Plausibility P : \mathcal{Q} \to [0,1], where P(q) is an estimate of the probability that the question’s premises are correct and its resolution is feasible. In practice, P is obtained from a calibrated ensemble or expert elicitation; here we treat it as a black‑box score.
· Accessibility A : \mathcal{Q} \to [0,1], where A(q) measures the proximity of q to existing human inference paths (Sourati & Evans, 2023). A value of 1 means the question is exactly on a well‑trodden path; 0 means it is far from human attention.

Definition D.1 (Novelty).

N(q) \;:=\; P(q) \cdot (1 - A(q)).

Proposition D.1. For all q \in \mathcal{Q}, 0 \le N(q) \le 1.

Proof. P(q) \in [0,1] and 1 - A(q) \in [0,1], so their product lies in [0,1]. ∎

Proposition D.2. If A(q) = 1, then N(q) = 0. If P(q) = 0, then N(q) = 0.

Proof. Substitution yields P(q) \cdot 0 = 0 and 0 \cdot (1-A(q)) = 0. ∎

Proposition D.3 (Monotonicity). For fixed accessibility A(q), novelty N(q) is strictly increasing in plausibility P(q) whenever A(q) < 1.

Proof. The derivative of N with respect to P is 1-A(q). If A(q) < 1, this is positive, so N increases with P. ∎

Remark D.4. The accessibility function requires a hypergraph of human inference paths. In a pilot domain with sparse literature, A(q) may be poorly estimated. The architecture must therefore include a confidence bound on A(q); if confidence is below a threshold, the question is flagged as “novelty uncertain” and may be excluded from high‑stakes decisions.

