Appendix E: Scoring and Pareto Frontier — Existence and Properties

Let S be a finite set of candidate questions. For each q \in S, define a score vector

\mathbf{v}(q) = (N(q),\, G(q),\, I(q),\, T(q),\, X(q),\, D(q),\, -C(q)) \in \mathbb{R}^7,
\]  

where the components correspond to novelty, grounded gap strength, importance, testability, cross‑domain potential, diversity contribution, and negative cost, respectively. All components are normalized to a common scale (e.g., [0,1]) before dominance is assessed.

Definition E.1 (Dominance). For q_1, q_2 \in S, q_1 dominates q_2, written q_1 \succ q_2, if

\forall i \in \{1,\ldots,7\},\; v_i(q_1) \ge v_i(q_2) \quad \text{and} \quad \exists j,\; v_j(q_1) > v_j(q_2).

Definition E.2 (Pareto Frontier). The Pareto frontier of S is

\mathcal{P}(S) = \{ q \in S \mid \nexists q' \in S \text{ with } q' \succ q \}.

Proposition E.1. If S is non‑empty and finite, then \mathcal{P}(S) is non‑empty.

Proof. Dominance \succ is a strict partial order on S: it is irreflexive and transitive (transitivity follows from the componentwise inequalities). In a finite partially ordered set, there exists at least one maximal element (by a simple induction on the size of S: remove an element; if the remaining set has a maximal element not dominated by the removed element, it remains maximal; otherwise the removed element itself is maximal). A maximal element is exactly one that is not dominated by any other, hence belongs to \mathcal{P}(S). ∎

Proposition E.2 (Lexicographic refinement). Any top element according to a lexicographic ordering of the score dimensions is in the Pareto frontier.

Proof. Suppose q^* is lexicographically maximal. If there existed q' \succ q^*, then q' would have all components \ge and at least one >. In the lexicographic order, the first dimension where they differ would have q' larger, contradicting maximality of q^*. Hence no such q' exists, so q^* \in \mathcal{P}(S). ∎

Selection rule. If computational resources require pruning the Pareto frontier, we apply a secondary criterion: maximize the information‑gain‑per‑unit‑cost ratio (Appendix F) or use a human‑selected subset.

