Appendix F: Information‑Theoretic Framework and Expected Information Gain

Let K be the random variable representing the true state of knowledge, and E the evidence available. The entropy H(K|E) quantifies uncertainty. For a question q with possible answers r \in \mathcal{R}_q (a finite set), the expected information gain is

IG(q) = H(K|E) - \sum_{r \in \mathcal{R}_q} P(r|E,q)\, H(K|E,r).

Proposition F.1. If the answer to q is already known with certainty, then IG(q) = 0.

Proof. If the answer is known, say r_0 with P(r_0|E,q) = 1, then the sum reduces to H(K|E,r_0). But H(K|E,r_0) = H(K|E) because r_0 is already part of E. Hence IG(q) = H(K|E) - H(K|E) = 0. ∎

Proposition F.2. For any question q, IG(q) \ge 0.

Proof. By the concavity of entropy (or the fact that conditioning reduces entropy),

\sum_{r} P(r|E,q) H(K|E,r) \le H(K|E).

Therefore IG(q) \ge 0. ∎

The optimal question maximizes

\frac{IG(q) \cdot Impact(q)}{Cost(q)}.

In the current architecture, this ideal is approximated by the scoring function (Section 11), with Impact mapped to importance I(q) and cross‑domain potential X(q), and Cost mapped to C(q).

