Appendix G: Loop Invariants and Consistency of the Epistemic Cycle

The closed discovery loop (Section 16) is a sequence of operations transforming the state (KG, M_G, M_Q). We identify several invariants that the system must maintain.

Invariant G.1 (Gap Genuineness). Every gap in M_G is genuine relative to the current KG: it is neither resolved nor dissolved, and it has survived the Gap Critic.

Proof. By induction on the loop index. Initially M_G=\emptyset. At each cycle, new gaps are added only after passing the Gap Critic, which checks for prior resolution, dissolution, and artifacts. After any KG update, existing gaps are re‑evaluated: if R or F becomes true, the gap is removed (or moved to a resolved/dissolved archive). Thus the invariant holds after every iteration. ∎

Invariant G.2 (Question Coverage). Every question ever generated by the system is recorded in M_Q with its current status.

Proof. The Question Engine (Stage 4–5) immediately inserts a record into M_Q upon generation. Subsequent stages only update the status field; they never delete records. Hence the invariant holds. ∎

Invariant G.3 (No Rediscovery). No question that is currently accepted (status = accepted) is a structural duplicate of a question already present in external literature or in M_Q with status accepted or resolved.

Proof. Before acceptance, the Critic performs external searches and compares structural signatures (Appendix H). If a match is found, the question is rejected or marked rediscovery. The comparison function is deterministic and transitive. Therefore, by induction, the set of accepted questions remains duplicate‑free. ∎

Invariant G.4 (Symbolic Core Integrity). All operations that modify KG, M_G, or M_Q are performed by the Symbolic Core according to well‑defined rules, and the Heuristic Periphery only supplies candidate inputs which are validated by the core.

Proof. This is an architectural guarantee enforced by the design: LLM outputs are never written directly to the symbolic memories; they are first converted into structured objects and passed through the appropriate verifier (Gap Critic, Question Critic, etc.). Thus the core remains consistent. ∎

