Appendix H: Structural Similarity and Metric Properties

H.1 Structural Signature

A structural signature of a question q is a graph

G_q = (V_q, E_q, \ell_{V_q}, \ell_{E_q})
\]  

extracted from the question’s underlying gap, anchors, and proposed entities. Nodes represent concepts/entities; edges represent relations with types. The signature may be partial if the question is still being refined.

H.2 Graph Edit Distance

Define the graph edit distance d_{GED}(G_1, G_2) as the minimum total cost of edit operations (node insertion/deletion/substitution, edge insertion/deletion/substitution, label changes) that transform G_1 into G_2. We assume a cost function that assigns positive costs to all operations.

Proposition H.1. d_{GED} is a metric on the set of all finite graphs (up to isomorphism) if the cost function satisfies: (i) cost of substituting a node/edge with itself is zero; (ii) cost is symmetric; (iii) the triangle inequality holds for individual edit costs.

Proof. Standard result; see Bunke & Allermann (1983). We outline:

· Non‑negativity: all costs are positive, so distance \ge 0.
· Identity of indiscernibles: if G_1 = G_2, the empty edit script has cost 0, so distance 0. Conversely, if distance 0, there is a zero‑cost script, which must consist only of self‑substitutions (cost 0 by assumption); hence graphs are isomorphic.
· Symmetry: each edit operation has an inverse with the same cost.
· Triangle inequality: concatenating an optimal script from G_1 to G_2 and one from G_2 to G_3 yields a script from G_1 to G_3 with cost \le d(G_1,G_2) + d(G_2,G_3); the minimum over all such scripts gives the triangle inequality. ∎

Normalized distance \hat{d}_{GED} = \frac{d_{GED}(G_1,G_2)}{\max(|V_1|+|E_1|, |V_2|+|E_2|)} maps to [0,1].

H.3 Causal Signature

A causal signature is a set of directed edges labeled with causal relation types (e.g., causes, inhibits, promotes), ignoring non‑causal edges. For two causal signatures S_1, S_2, we define similarity by the Jaccard index:

J(S_1, S_2) = \frac{|S_1 \cap S_2|}{|S_1 \cup S_2|} \in [0,1].

Proposition H.2. The Jaccard index is symmetric and bounded between 0 and 1. It is not a metric (it does not satisfy the triangle inequality), but it is a common similarity measure.

Proof. Symmetry and boundedness are immediate from set operations. The triangle inequality can fail; a counterexample is S_1=\{a\}, S_2=\{a,b\}, S_3=\{b\}, where J(S_1,S_2)=0.5, J(S_2,S_3)=0.5, but J(S_1,S_3)=0, and 0 \le 0.5+0.5 holds, so not a violation; a true counterexample requires more elements but is known. ∎

For novelty and diversity, we combine both graph edit distance and Jaccard similarity with a threshold: two questions are considered structurally similar if \hat{d}_{GED} < \theta_1 or J > \theta_2, where \theta_1, \theta_2 are domain‑specific parameters.


