I.5 Gap Detection Patterns (for each type)

I.5.1 Contradiction Gap

```
  Source 1                  Source 2
     |                         |
     v                         v
  Edge1: e1 supports H      Edge2: e2 supports ¬H
     |                         |
     +--------->  ?  <---------+
                 |
        Reconciliation attempt:
        If contextual variable C explains conflict,
        add C as edge condition and dissolve gap.
        Else store gap.
```

I.5.2 Missing Mechanism Gap

```
  A  ----empirically-observed---->  B
              |
              v
      No node m of type "mechanism"
      with edge m --explains--> (A -> B)
      -> Gap stored, unless prior search found
         evidence of absence (active search returned nothing).
```

I.5.3 Boundary Gap

```
  Model M valid on domain D
         |
         v
  Adjacent domain B (where M fails)
  -> Gap: "At what conditions does M fail?"
```

I.5.4 Anomaly Gap

```
  Predicted y_pred vs Observed y_obs
       |
       v
  |y_obs - y_pred| > ε across multiple conditions
  -> Systematic deviation, not random -> Gap.
```

I.5.5 Cross-Domain Gap

```
  Domain A: Mechanism M_A
       |
       v
  Structural similarity (partial graph isomorphism)
       |
       v
  Domain B: Problem P_B
  -> Transfer candidate accepted only if relation types preserved.
```

I.5.6 Neglect Gap

```
  Plausibility high, but Accessibility low
       |
       v
  Area is important but weakly investigated
  (adopted from Sourati & Evans, 2023)
  -> Gap of type Neglect.
```

Explanation: Each gap type has a distinctive pattern that the Gap Engine searches for. The patterns are formalized as graph queries in the symbolic core.

